<div class="header">
  <h1>AI Briefing Quality Metrics</h1>
  <div class="sub">What each metric means and why it matters for your dispatch decisions</div>
</div>

<div class="content">

  <a href="report.html" class="back-link">&larr; Back to Dispatch Board</a>

  <div class="intro">
    <h2>Why we measure AI quality</h2>
    <p>
      Every time this tool runs, the AI writes a morning briefing summarizing your alert data.
      Before that briefing reaches you, we automatically check it against the same data the
      scoring engine uses — the "ground truth." The scorecard on the main report tells you
      how trustworthy this particular briefing is, so you know when to rely on it and when
      to look at the tables instead.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- FAITHFULNESS -->
  <div class="metric-card">
    <div class="metric-header">
      <div class="metric-icon" style="background:#16a34a">F</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Faithfulness</h3>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#6b7280">Are the AI's claims actually true?</div>
      </div>
      <span class="weight">Weight: 30%</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-body">
      <h4>What it checks</h4>
      <p>
        When the AI says "V-137 is at North Yard" or "V-108 had a post-repair fault after WO-5512,"
        we verify those claims against the actual data. Does V-137 really belong to North Yard?
        Was WO-5512 really the work order for V-108? Every factual claim the AI makes is
        cross-referenced.
      </p>

      <h4>Why it matters</h4>
      <p>
        If the AI puts V-137 at the wrong depot, the Hamilton maintenance lead wastes time looking
        for a truck that's actually at North Yard. Wrong facts lead to wrong decisions.
      </p>

      <div class="example good">
        <strong>100% score:</strong> "V-137 (North Yard) has an oil pressure fault after WO-5540 repair."
        — Every fact matches the data.
      </div>
      <div class="example bad">
        <strong>Low score:</strong> "V-137 (Airport Hub) needs attention." — Wrong depot.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- HALLUCINATION -->
  <div class="metric-card">
    <div class="metric-header">
      <div class="metric-icon" style="background:#dc2626">H</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Hallucination</h3>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#6b7280">Did the AI make things up?</div>
      </div>
      <span class="weight">Weight: 25%</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-body">
      <h4>What it checks</h4>
      <p>
        We verify that every vehicle ID (V-101 to V-160), driver ID (D-01 to D-60),
        work order number, and fault code the AI mentions actually exists in the data.
        We also check that the AI doesn't call a vehicle a "dispatch blocker" when it
        isn't one — that's a hallucinated status, not just a hallucinated name.
      </p>

      <h4>Why it matters</h4>
      <p>
        This is the most dangerous failure. If the AI says "V-270 needs immediate attention"
        and V-270 doesn't exist, someone wastes time looking for a ghost truck. Worse, if it
        says "V-145 must not dispatch" when V-145 is actually fine, you lose a truck from
        your fleet for no reason. We weight this 25% of the total score.
      </p>

      <div class="example good">
        <strong>100% score:</strong> All mentioned vehicles, drivers, WOs, and DTCs exist in the data.
        No false blockers identified.
      </div>
      <div class="example bad">
        <strong>Low score:</strong> AI mentions "V-270" (doesn't exist) or "D-53" (out of range)
        or says V-145 is a blocker (it isn't).
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- COMPLETENESS -->
  <div class="metric-card">
    <div class="metric-header">
      <div class="metric-icon" style="background:#d97706">C</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Completeness</h3>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#6b7280">Did the AI mention everything important?</div>
      </div>
      <span class="weight">Weight: 20%</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-body">
      <h4>What it checks</h4>
      <p>
        The AI should mention every dispatch blocker, every post-repair fault, and key data
        quality facts (like duplicates removed and vendor severity discrepancies). We count
        how many of these critical items appear in the briefing.
      </p>

      <h4>Why it matters</h4>
      <p>
        A briefing that mentions V-137 but forgets V-108 could lead you to dispatch a truck
        with two critical engine faults. The whole point of the briefing is to flag everything
        that needs attention — missing an item defeats the purpose.
      </p>

      <div class="example good">
        <strong>100% score:</strong> All 4 blockers mentioned, both post-repair faults called out,
        duplicate count noted, vendor overcount flagged.
      </div>
      <div class="example bad">
        <strong>67% score:</strong> Mentions 3 of 4 blockers and skips V-108.
        The ops manager might dispatch V-108 without knowing about the overheating.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- NUMERICAL ACCURACY -->
  <div class="metric-card">
    <div class="metric-header">
      <div class="metric-icon" style="background:#1d4ed8">N</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Numerical Accuracy</h3>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#6b7280">Are the numbers right?</div>
      </div>
      <span class="weight">Weight: 15%</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-body">
      <h4>What it checks</h4>
      <p>
        When the AI says "2,380 raw alerts" or "134 duplicates removed" or "42 HIGH-priority,"
        we compare those numbers to the actual counts from the scoring engine. We allow a 15%
        tolerance (rounding and paraphrasing can shift numbers slightly), but anything beyond
        that is flagged.
      </p>

      <h4>Why it matters</h4>
      <p>
        If the AI says "30% duplicates" when the actual rate is 6%, it creates a false
        impression that the data quality is much worse than it is. Numbers build trust —
        wrong numbers destroy it.
      </p>

      <div class="example good">
        <strong>100% score:</strong> "134 duplicates removed from 2,380 alerts" — matches exactly.
      </div>
      <div class="example bad">
        <strong>Low score:</strong> "30% duplicates" when actual is ~6%, or "5,000 raw alerts"
        when actual is 2,380.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- RELEVANCE -->
  <div class="metric-card">
    <div class="metric-header">
      <div class="metric-icon" style="background:#7c3aed">R</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Relevance</h3>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#6b7280">Does the AI focus on what matters most?</div>
      </div>
      <span class="weight">Weight: 10%</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-body">
      <h4>What it checks</h4>
      <p>
        Dispatch blockers should appear before lower-priority action items. Post-repair faults
        (the most surprising and dangerous findings) should be emphasized. If the AI discusses
        driver behaviour, it should include the firmware context that explains why some drivers'
        numbers look worse than they are.
      </p>

      <h4>Why it matters</h4>
      <p>
        If the briefing leads with an idling alert and buries the "V-137 can't dispatch" note
        at the bottom, the ops manager might skim the top and miss the critical action. The
        order of information shapes decisions.
      </p>

      <div class="example good">
        <strong>High score:</strong> Leads with blockers, then post-repair faults, then action items.
      </div>
      <div class="example bad">
        <strong>Low score:</strong> Leads with "327 idling alerts" before mentioning any blocker.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- GRADE TABLE -->
  <div class="metric-card">
    <div class="metric-header">
      <h3>Overall Grade Scale</h3>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-body">
      <table class="grade-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Grade</th><th>Score</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="font-weight:700;color:#16a34a">A</td><td>90%+</td>
              <td>Briefing is reliable. Safe to read and act on as-is.</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="font-weight:700;color:#65a30d">B</td><td>80-89%</td>
              <td>Good overall, but double-check any items the scorecard flagged.</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="font-weight:700;color:#d97706">C</td><td>65-79%</td>
              <td>Some gaps or inaccuracies. Use the tables below the briefing as your primary source.</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="font-weight:700;color:#ea580c">D</td><td>50-64%</td>
              <td>Significant problems. Rely on the tables, not the briefing text.</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="font-weight:700;color:#dc2626">F</td><td>Below 50%</td>
              <td>Briefing is unreliable. It was likely replaced with the template fallback.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- TRUST BOX -->
  <div class="trust-box">
    <h3>The bottom line</h3>
    <p>
      The AI briefing is a convenience — a 60-second summary so you don't have to read
      2,000+ alert rows. But every number in the stats bar, every vehicle in the blockers
      list, and every row in the action items comes from the <strong>deterministic scoring
      engine</strong>, not the AI. That engine is tested with 79 automated checks and
      produces the same output every time for the same data.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top:12px">
      When the AI scores an A, the briefing is a trustworthy narrative. When it scores lower,
      you know exactly which dimension failed and can look at the tables instead. Either way,
      the data is always there.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:12px;text-align:center;padding:32px 0">
    Maple Freight AI Evaluation Guide &middot; Generated alongside the Dispatch Decision Board
  </div>

</div>
