<div class="header">
  <h1>AI Briefing Quality Metrics</h1>
  <div class="sub">What each metric means and why it matters for your dispatch decisions</div>
</div>

<div class="content">

  <a href="report.html" class="back-link">&larr; Back to Dispatch Board</a>

  <div class="intro">
    <h2>Why we measure AI quality</h2>
    <p>
      Every time this tool runs, the AI writes a morning briefing summarizing your alert data.
      Before that briefing reaches you, we automatically check it against the same data the
      scoring engine uses. The scorecard on the main report tells you
      how trustworthy this particular briefing is, so you know when to rely on it and when
      to look at the tables instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      The AI doesn't search for information (this isn't a search engine). It reads the structured
      data our scoring engine produces and writes a narrative summary of it. The metrics below
      measure how well that summary represents the data it was given.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="how-box">
    <h2>How the pipeline works</h2>
    <div class="pipeline">
      <div class="pipeline-step">Alert CSVs<br><span style="font-weight:400;font-size:12px;color:#6b7280">2,380 raw alerts</span></div>
      <div class="pipeline-arrow">&rarr;</div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">Scoring Engine<br><span style="font-weight:400;font-size:12px;color:#6b7280">deterministic rules</span></div>
      <div class="pipeline-arrow">&rarr;</div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">Structured Summary<br><span style="font-weight:400;font-size:12px;color:#6b7280">blockers, scores, stats</span></div>
      <div class="pipeline-arrow">&rarr;</div>
      <div class="pipeline-step" style="background:#fef9c3;border-color:#ca8a04">AI Model<br><span style="font-weight:400;font-size:12px;color:#6b7280">writes the narrative</span></div>
      <div class="pipeline-arrow">&rarr;</div>
      <div class="pipeline-step" style="background:#f0fdf4;border-color:#16a34a">Eval Check<br><span style="font-weight:400;font-size:12px;color:#6b7280">4 metrics scored</span></div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:13px;color:#6b7280;text-align:center">
      The eval compares the AI's narrative (step 4) back against the structured summary (step 3).
      The scoring engine's output is always the source of truth.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- GROUNDEDNESS -->
  <div class="metric-card">
    <div class="metric-header">
      <div class="metric-icon" style="background:#16a34a">G</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Groundedness</h3>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#6b7280">Is every claim traceable to the data?</div>
      </div>
      <span class="weight">Weight: 35%</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-body">
      <h4>What it checks</h4>
      <p>
        Two things: <strong>entity grounding</strong> (every vehicle, driver, work order, and fault
        code the AI mentions actually exists in the data) and <strong>relation grounding</strong>
        (associations like "V-137 at North Yard" or "V-108 is a dispatch blocker" match reality).
      </p>

      <h4>Why it's the highest weight (35%)</h4>
      <p>
        A briefing that mentions trucks that don't exist, or says a truck is grounded when it's not,
        causes real operational damage. This is the most dangerous way an AI can fail: making up facts
        that look authoritative. We give this the highest weight because a single fabricated vehicle
        or false blocker status can derail the entire morning dispatch.
      </p>

      <div class="example good">
        <strong>High score:</strong> "V-137 (North Yard) has an oil pressure fault after WO-5540 repair."
        — Every entity exists, the depot is correct, the work order matches.
      </div>
      <div class="example bad">
        <strong>Low score:</strong> "V-270 needs immediate attention" (V-270 doesn't exist),
        or "V-145 must not dispatch" (V-145 is not actually a blocker).
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- COVERAGE -->
  <div class="metric-card">
    <div class="metric-header">
      <div class="metric-icon" style="background:#d97706">C</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Coverage</h3>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#6b7280">Did the AI mention everything important?</div>
      </div>
      <span class="weight">Weight: 25%</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-body">
      <h4>What it checks</h4>
      <p>
        The AI should mention every dispatch blocker, every post-repair fault, and key data
        quality facts (duplicates removed, vendor severity discrepancies). We count how many
        of these critical items from the scoring engine's output appear in the briefing.
      </p>

      <h4>Why it matters</h4>
      <p>
        A briefing that mentions V-137 but forgets V-108 could lead you to dispatch a truck
        with two critical engine faults. The whole point of the briefing is to surface everything
        that needs attention — a gap in coverage is a gap in your decision-making.
      </p>

      <div class="example good">
        <strong>High score:</strong> All 4 blockers mentioned, both post-repair faults called out,
        duplicate count noted, vendor overcount flagged.
      </div>
      <div class="example bad">
        <strong>Low score:</strong> Mentions 2 of 4 blockers and skips the Hamilton backfill vehicles entirely.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- CORRECTNESS -->
  <div class="metric-card">
    <div class="metric-header">
      <div class="metric-icon" style="background:#1d4ed8">N</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Correctness</h3>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#6b7280">Are the numbers and facts right?</div>
      </div>
      <span class="weight">Weight: 25%</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-body">
      <h4>What it checks</h4>
      <p>
        When the AI says "2,380 raw alerts" or "134 duplicates removed" or "42 HIGH-priority,"
        we compare those numbers to the actual counts from the scoring engine. We also catch
        false claims like "no duplicates found" when there were 134, or "30% duplicates" when
        the real rate is about 6%.
      </p>

      <h4>Why it matters</h4>
      <p>
        Numbers build trust. If the AI says "30% duplicates" when the actual rate is 6%, it
        creates a false impression that the data quality is terrible — which could undermine
        confidence in the whole system. Getting the numbers right is table stakes for credibility.
      </p>

      <div class="example good">
        <strong>High score:</strong> "134 duplicates removed from 2,380 alerts, 42 ranked HIGH" — matches exactly.
      </div>
      <div class="example bad">
        <strong>Low score:</strong> "30% duplicates" (actual ~6%), or "5,000 raw alerts" (actual 2,380).
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ACTIONABILITY -->
  <div class="metric-card">
    <div class="metric-header">
      <div class="metric-icon" style="background:#7c3aed">A</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Actionability</h3>
        <div style="font-size:13px;color:#6b7280">Does it help you make tonight's decisions?</div>
      </div>
      <span class="weight">Weight: 15%</span>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-body">
      <h4>What it checks</h4>
      <p>
        Three things: (1) dispatch blockers should appear <strong>before</strong> lower-priority items,
        because you need to know what can't go out before planning what can;
        (2) post-repair faults should be called out, because those are the most surprising
        and dangerous findings; (3) the briefing should include <strong>next-step language</strong>
        ("inspect," "do not dispatch," "open a work order") — not just describe the situation.
      </p>

      <h4>Why it matters</h4>
      <p>
        A briefing can be perfectly accurate but still useless if it buries the "V-137 can't dispatch"
        note at the bottom and leads with idling statistics. The order and framing of information
        shapes decisions. This metric checks whether the AI organised its output to actually help
        you act, not just to report.
      </p>

      <div class="example good">
        <strong>High score:</strong> Leads with blockers, calls out post-repair faults, tells you
        to "re-inspect V-137 before dispatch" and "open a work order for V-108."
      </div>
      <div class="example bad">
        <strong>Low score:</strong> Leads with "327 idling alerts" and only mentions blockers in passing
        at the end. No next steps suggested.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- GRADE TABLE -->
  <div class="metric-card">
    <div class="metric-header">
      <h3>Overall Grade Scale</h3>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-body">
      <table class="grade-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Grade</th><th>Score</th><th>What it means for you</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td style="font-weight:700;color:#16a34a">A</td><td>90%+</td>
              <td>Briefing is reliable. Safe to read and act on as-is.</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="font-weight:700;color:#65a30d">B</td><td>80-89%</td>
              <td>Good overall, but double-check any items the scorecard flagged.</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="font-weight:700;color:#d97706">C</td><td>65-79%</td>
              <td>Gaps or inaccuracies. Use the tables below the briefing as your primary source.</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="font-weight:700;color:#ea580c">D</td><td>50-64%</td>
              <td>Significant problems. Rely on the tables, not the briefing text.</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="font-weight:700;color:#dc2626">F</td><td>Below 50%</td>
              <td>Briefing is unreliable. Was likely replaced with the template fallback.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- TRUST BOX -->
  <div class="trust-box">
    <h3>The bottom line</h3>
    <p>
      The AI briefing is a convenience — a 60-second summary so you don't have to read
      2,000+ alert rows. But every number in the stats bar, every vehicle in the blockers
      list, and every row in the action items comes from the <strong>deterministic scoring
      engine</strong>, not the AI. That engine is tested with 79 automated checks and
      produces the same output every time for the same data.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top:12px">
      When the AI scores an A, the briefing is a trustworthy narrative. When it scores lower,
      you know exactly which dimension failed and can look at the tables instead. Either way,
      the data is always there.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="color:#9ca3af;font-size:12px;text-align:center;padding:32px 0">
    Maple Freight AI Evaluation Guide &middot; Generated alongside the Dispatch Decision Board
  </div>

</div>
</body>
</html>
